\begin_layout Standard
The coexistence of these two powerful computational ecosystems creates a fragmentation problem.
 A typical modern genomic workflow might require the robust methods available in Bioconductor (R),
 followed by the application of deep learning models or inference methods available in Python.
 Researchers traditionally bridge this divide by exchanging data using file based formats or through reticulate,
 e.g.
 csv,
 h5,
 BiocPy addresses this gap rather than forcing R to run inside Python (or vice-versa) or creating an entirely new,
 incompatible Python-native standard,
 which loses information and incurs serialization costs.
 Reimplementing statistical methods in Python often leads to subtle algorithmic divergence and incompatibility with standard data models.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
BiocPy ports Bioconductor’s core class definitions into Python.
 A 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
SummarizedExperiment
\end_layout

\end_inset

 object in BiocPy is designed to have the same slots,
 and the same behavioral guarantees as its R counterpart.
 This ensures that the conceptual data model remains consistent,
 reducing the cognitive burden for researchers and developers transitioning between languages.
 By adhering to Bioconductor’s design principles,
 such as the separation of assay data from row/column metadata and the use of functional programming patterns,
 BiocPy enables a workflow where data can round-trip between R and Python.
 These create a bridge between computational platforms and enable researchers to focus their time on biological questions rather than technical challenges.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The BiocPy framework is targeted at developers who require the reliability of Bioconductor's data structures within a Python environment.
 BiocPy is not the only attempt to solve the R/Python divide,
 but its approach is unique.
 Unlike rpy2
