\begin_layout Subsubsection
Read Serialized RDS files
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A major barrier in cross-language workflows is the incompatibility of data serialization formats (R’s RDS vs.
 Python’s Pickle).
 BiocPy provides rds2py
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "noauthor_biocpyrds2py_2025"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 a package that can read R’s binary serialization format (RDS).
 This allows Python environments to ingest complex S4 objects directly from disk without requiring an installed R runtime.
 With the availability of Bioconductor classes in Python,
 we can provide access to the same data from R.
 biocpy classes facilkuitate makes we can accurately to represent the objects inside RDS objects within a python environment with minimal information loss.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Language-Agnostic Formats with ArtifactDB
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For sustainable collaborative workflows across computational environments,
 BiocPy integrates with language-agnostic schemas and specifications defined in ArtifactDB
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "noauthor_artifactdb_nodate"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 ArtifactDB is a system for managing genomic datasets on-disk where data is stored in language-neutral formats (HDF5 for matrices,
 JSON for metadata etc) but is wrapped in rich semantic metadata.
 ArtifactDB validates these objects across R and Python to ensure cross-language accessibility of the data.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
An R user can serialize a to disk using ArtifactDB,
 and a Python user can fetch that same artifact from disk.
 The ArtifactDB python clients automatically reconstruct the native Python object from disk using an appropriate BiocPy class.
 This ensures that datasets remain consistent across the research community,
 regardless of the programming language used for analysis.
\end_layout

